<https://bio.tools/biotools:cluefish> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "Cluefish" ;
  dc:description """Cluefish is an R workflow designed for comprehensive biological interpretation of transcriptomic data series, applying over-representation analysis on pre-clustered protein-protein interaction networks to identify specific biological functions. It's freely available at GitHub and aims to provide valuable clues for biological hypothesis generation."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://ellfran-7.github.io/cluefish/articles/cluefish.html> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:algicidedb> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "AlgicideDB" ;
  dc:description """AlgicideDB is a database containing information on algicides targeting harmful algal blooms, incorporating a large language model for query assistance. The platform facilitates the exploration of algicidal compounds and aims to support the development of safe and effective HAB control strategies."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://algicidedb.ocean-meta.com/#/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:doserider> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "DoseRider" ;
  dc:description """DoseRider is an R package and web application that models dose-response relationships at the pathway level using generalized mixed effect models. It calculates trend change doses (TCDs) and benchmark doses (BMDs) to understand how compounds affect biological systems at various doses."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://doserider.i-med.ac.at> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:intob> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "INTOB" ;
  dc:description """INTOB is a software resource developed by the UFZ for the collection and management of toxicological data, adhering to FAIR principles. It standardizes the collection of metadata and phenotypic observations, particularly for zebrafish embryo toxicity tests, to improve data comparability and facilitate data-driven analyses."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://www.ufz.de/intob/index.php?en=51326> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:pretoxtm> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "PretoxTM" ;
  dc:description """PretoxTM is a text mining system designed to extract treatment-related findings from preclinical toxicology reports. The tool comprises a corpus, pipeline, and web app, and uses models fine-tuned on the PretoxTM dataset."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://huggingface.co/datasets/javicorvi/pretoxtm-dataset> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:toxprint> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "ToxPrint" ;
  dc:description """ToxPrint is a publicly available invariant reference set (chemotype library) designed to cover chemical structures from toxicity databases. It's used for analytical quality evaluation of compound libraries, as demonstrated in a recent publication."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://toxprint.org/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:bridgedb> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "BridgeDb" ;
  dc:description """BridgeDb is a framework to map identifiers between various databases.  It includes a Java library that provides an API to work with identifier-identifier mapping databases and resources."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> ;
  foaf:page <http://bridgedb.github.io/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:ctdquerier> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "CTDquerier" ;
  dc:description """Comparative Toxicogenomics Database data extraction, visualization and enrichment of environmental and toxicological studies."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://github.com/isglobal-brge/CTDquerier> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:jecfa> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "JECFA Data Retriever" ;
  dc:description """The JECFA Data Retriever is an R package and associated workflow that automates the retrieval of key identifiers and toxicological data from the Joint FAO/WHO Expert Committee on Food Additives (JECFA) database via web scraping. It provides a reproducible pipeline for accessing and processing JECFA data, facilitating more efficient research."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://ubesp-dctv.github.io/jecfa/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:toxdar> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "ToxDAR" ;
  dc:description """ToxDAR is an R package designed for analyzing toxicological multi-omics data, integrating packages like DESeq2 and igraph, and providing a knowledge graph for data annotation and pathway analysis."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://github.com/TMCjp/ToxDAR> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:geotox> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "GeoTox" ;
  dc:description """GeoTox is an open-source R software package for characterizing the risk of perturbing molecular targets involved in adverse human health outcomes based on exposure to spatially-referenced stressor mixtures. It facilitates source-to-outcome continuum modeling for individual and population-level risk assessment."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://niehs.github.io/GeoTox/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:manamodeller> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "MANA (mMoA identification Assisted by modelling and Network Analysis)" ;
  dc:description """MANA is a Python module that detects metabolic changes induced by chemical exposure by computing condition-specific metabolic models and analyzing their impact on metabolic networks. It provides a workflow to identify mechanisms of action (mMoA) through network analysis and visualization of metabolic changes."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://pypi.org/project/manamodeller/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:marigold> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "Marigold" ;
  dc:description """Marigold is a free and open-source web application for analyzing zebrafish behavior, featuring a graphical user interface for tracking keypoints and exporting kinematic parameters. It leverages a custom neural network architecture for efficient pose tracking, accessible through standard web browsers."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://downeslab.github.io/marigold/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:cpsign> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "CPSign" ;
  dc:description """CPSign is an open-source software tool for quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) modeling utilizing conformal and probabilistic prediction. It supports several chemical descriptors and modeling methods, including SVM, with features for visualizing results and deploying models as REST services."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://cpsign.readthedocs.io> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:pfas> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "PFAS Systematic Evidence Map (Shiny App)" ;
  dc:description """The PFAS Systematic Evidence Map is a research synthesis and interactive database providing a systematic review of the literature on PFAS exposure. It provides a visual map, database, and analytical tools to guide research and facilitate collaboration."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://hi-this-is-lorenzo.shinyapps.io/PFAS_SEM_Shiny_App/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:mitotouch> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "MitoTouch" ;
  dc:description """MitoTouch is a computerized method for analyzing mitochondrial shape and network architecture from confocal fluorescence images, providing more than thirty features to quantify changes in mitochondrial organization. It enables the identification of subtle differences in morphology and has potential applications in cell biology, medicine, and environmental toxicology."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://github.com/quantacell/MitoTouch> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:qsar-ready> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "QSAR-ready workflow" ;
  dc:description """This is a free and open-source workflow for standardizing chemical structures in preparation for QSAR modeling. It is implemented in KNIME and available as a Docker container."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <https://hub.docker.com/r/kamelmansouri/qsar-ready> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:dbeth> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "DBETH" ;
  dc:description """Database of sequences, structures, interaction networks and analytical results for 229 exotoxins, from 26 different human pathogenic bacterial genus. All toxins are classified into 24 different Toxin classes. The aim is to provide a comprehensive database for human pathogenic bacterial exotoxins."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://www.hpppi.iicb.res.in/btox/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:limtox> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "LimTox" ;
  dc:description """Text mining for toxicology."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://limtox.bioinfo.cnio.es/> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:t3db> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "T3DB" ;
  dc:description """Unique bioinformatics resource that compiles comprehensive information about common or ubiquitous toxins and their toxin-targets. Each record (ToxCard) contains over 80 data fields providing detailed information on chemical properties and descriptors, toxicity values, protein and gene sequences (for both targets and toxins), molecular and cellular interaction data, toxicological data, mechanistic information and references."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://www.t3db.org> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:toxsign> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "TOXsIgN" ;
  dc:description """The TOXicogenomic sIgNature (TOXsIgN) is a cross-species resource that supports online submission, storage, and retrieval of TOXicogenomic sIgNatures. TOXsIgN aims at complementing existing resources by acting as a distribution hub for the community. One of its unique features is its ability to archive heterogeneous data. TOXsIgN thus allows users to upload lists of genes positively (overexpressed for transcriptomic assays) affected and negatively (underexpressed) affected from distinct –omics experiments (e.g., transcriptomics, proteomics, or epigenomics). It also provides users with a working environment containing a powerful search engine as well as bioinformatics/biostatistics modules that enable cross-species and cross-technology signature comparisons or enrichment analyses. TOXsIgN is thus intended to serve as a warehouse for toxicogenomics and predictive toxicology tools simultaneously based on and able to analyze the overall set of signatures deposited by the community."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://toxsign.genouest.org> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:masi> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "MASI" ;
  dc:description """Microbiota-active substance interactions database.
Active Substance Interaction database.
Try Examples: Bifidobacterium adolescentis, Enterococcus faecalis, Acarbose, Caffeic acid, Oxaprozin, Obesity, Colorectal cancer.

A centralized and standardized database to catalog Microbiota-Active Substance Interaction (MASI) will be very useful for related research fields, including microbiota-related precision medicine, pharmaceutical research, food and nutrition research, clinical pharmacy, health management studies.

Accumulating evidence about the interaction between human microbiota and active substances, especially for those therapeutically-relevant substances such as clinical used drugs and traditional medicines/herbs, is deepenning our understanding in the role of "our second genome" in human health and diseases, and opens new avenue for managing/treating human healthy problems/diseases by manipulating microbiota-human interactions."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://www.aiddlab.com/MASI> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:fastbmd> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "FastBMD" ;
  dc:description """FastBMD is an online tool for rapid benchmark dose-response analysis of transcriptomics data. FastBMD is a computationally efficient implementation of the National Toxicology Program Approach to Genomic Dose-Response Modeling. Key features include a simplified workflow, ability to download results at each step, and interactive exploration of pathway-level BMDs. In addition to model organisms, FastBMD is designed to support non-model organisms by enabling an annotation-free pipeline that includes feature sensitivity-based point-of-departure analysis. The final part of the analysis is designed to allow users to interactively explore BMDs at different levels of organization:."""@en ;
  dct:license <http://example.com/unknown> ;
  foaf:page <http://www.fastbmd.ca> .
  

<https://bio.tools/biotools:genra-py> a sbd:Resource ;
  dc:source <https://bio.tools/t?domain=toxicology> ;
  rdfs:label "genra-py" ;
  dc:description """genra-py is a Python package for GenRA. Genralised Read-Across (GenRA) is a computational toxicology tool to mimic a human expert’s manual reasoning based on similarity-weighted activity."""@en ;
  dct:license <https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT> ;
  foaf:page <http://github.com/i-shah/genra-py> .
  

